{
  "_why": "wait_threads is the only blocking conversation tool, so its wake rules are pinned here rather than left to prose. The rules decide when a coordinator stops waiting, and getting one wrong is silent: too eager and every poll wakes on unchanged work, too lazy and a thread parked on an approval never gets noticed. A target wakes on a COMPLETED turn, on an actionable status (waitingOnApproval / waitingOnUserInput), or on an inactive status (systemError / notLoaded). An active target with no flags is progressing and must NOT wake — commentary never wakes a wait. A cursor identifies the last reported turn, so an unchanged terminal turn is suppressed instead of being reported forever; an absent cursor means nothing was seen yet, so everything is a change. The cursor is deliberately tolerant: a corrupted or hand-written value degrades to 'nothing seen' rather than failing the wait. Suppressed text is withheld (null) while the snapshot still carries the advanced cursor, so a caller that passes it back gets only new work. A target whose latest turn reached a terminal state reports turnCompleted even when its status is notLoaded or systemError, because a finished turn is concrete evidence while notLoaded is merely the engine's default for a thread with durable history and no live status — the normal state of a sidebar thread nobody has open. Ranking the status above the turn evidence would tell a coordinator that completed work needs a human. An active-with-flags target still outranks both, since it is genuinely blocked on a person. Host IDs now address same-account peers. Unknown or offline peers produce per-target errors at execution time; tuple identity and remote wait behavior are pinned in remote-thread-tools-vectors.json. Wait replies expose preview truncation and a read action for newly completed turns. Collection preserves the reported turn identity; unchanged completions stay suppressed and progress previews do not imply completion. Actionable flags participate in active-turn cursors so a target entering approval or user-input wait during the same turn wakes once; legacy and terminal cursor encodings remain accepted.",
